udence and verifiable facts, while the bull's case, though exciting, relies heavily on narrative and speculation. The single most compelling point for me was the bear's emphasis on the complete lack of fundamental data. We have no P/E ratio, no revenue figures, no cash flow statements to analyze. The bull is asking us to invest in a story about an IPO and an acquisition. The bear is reminding us that we have no idea if the underlying business is actually healthy enough to support this story.  Furthermore, the bear's risk analysis is far more thorough. The dependency on Apple is a massive, undeniable risk that the bull dismisses as a strength. In today's geopolitical climate, that's a dangerously concentrated bet. And while the short-term technicals are positive, the context is critical. A rally into a long-term downtrend, with the RSI heating up, is more often a trap for latecomers than an opportunity for smart money. The 200-day moving average isn't just a line on a chart; it represents the long-term consensus on value, and right now, that consensus is still bearish. We can't ignore that.  So, here is our investment plan for the trader.  First, our recommendation is a clear Sell. If we have any existing long positions in 002475, we will exit them at the market open. The risk of this rally failing is simply too high to justify holding on. If we are flat, we will not be initiating a new long position.  Second, we will remain on the sidelines but keep this stock on our watchlist. I'm not saying this company is a bad company forever, but I am saying it's a bad investment *right now*. For me to turn bullish, I would need to see two things happen. First, the price would need to decisively break above the 200-day moving average and, more importantly, hold above it for several trading sessions on significant volume. That would prove the market is serious about re-rating the stock. Second, and this is non-negotiable, the narrative needs to be backed by numbers. After this I